d a recent GOP presidential win — and with no polls or expert ratings available, there is little basis to move from that picture. That leaves a genuine tossup with a slight Republican tilt and unusually wide uncertainty.", "watch": "The first credible statewide polls and the final identity of each party's nominee could quickly shift this tossup in either direction.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["No expert ratings published yet; forecast rests on fundamentals and polls.", "New or vacant seat with no expert ratings; fundamentals only.", "No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings."]}, "overview": "Arizona remains one of the closest presidential battlegrounds, with no incumbent on the ballot and no public general-election polling yet. The state's partisan lean sits about two points to the right of the nation, and Republicans carried it in the most recent presidential contest, though Democrats have won it within the past decade. That recent history keeps the state squarely in tossup territory with a slight Republican tilt.\n\nThe 2026 midterms offered some signals: Trump-endorsed candidates prevailed in Arizona's primaries, and several Republican nominees for state office have questioned election results, themes that could shape turnout on both sides. Democrats, meanwhile, boast prominent in-state figures drawing national attention. Until polling arrives, fundamentals point to a narrow Republican edge with wide uncertainty.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.4092, "margin": -2.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.4294, "margin": -2.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.4273, "margin": -2.0}]}